Register-resident softmax for the SM75 verify-attention kernel.

The kernel kept the QK scores in a 4.6 KiB shared tile: each warp wrote its
16x16 score block, a barrier crossed, and a different warp (rows are owned
per warp in the softmax loop) reloaded full 32-column rows to reduce over 32
lanes with five shuffles. Measured, that store/reload exchange plus the
masking was 45% of the layer (save_score 16%, softmax 29% at 131k keys / five
queries / 32 segments, one layer).

Here the scores stay in the QK accumulators (four values per lane per row).
Masking and the per-column K scale are applied in registers, the reduction
runs over the four lanes that hold a row's half-row (two shuffles, all eight
row groups at once), and the two warps that split a row exchange one (max,
sum) pair per row through 512 B of shared. Probabilities are written straight
to s.probability for the PV step; there is no score buffer.

Reduction order changes, so the output is numerically equivalent, not bitwise
identical: max |new - old| = 7.6e-6 on the packed fixture, relative RMS 4e-4
(the baseline is 5.7e-4 against the dense reference), and the dense check
holds at 5.3e-4 with cosine 1.0.

Measured on 131k keys, five queries, 32 segments, one layer: 1.726 -> 1.223 ms
(-29%). End to end on the 2080 Ti at 262k/5.5 GiB, same seed and prompts,
step time 41.2 -> 39.0 ms at 32k, 46.6 -> 44.0 ms at 64k, 57.0 -> 52.9 ms at
128k (-5.4% / -5.6% / -7.2%), growing with the attention share. Greedy
acceptance shifts because near-ties flip on the changed rounding.

